 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Does fashion always have a duty to serve up fairytales? Must designers treat their shows as a vessel to teach us lessons of morality and to make sense of this crazy, complicated world? Not all the time: Sometimes, it’s fine just to make great clothes. But this season, Christian Siriano was feeling enchanted by the classic 18th-century French tale “The Story of the Queen of the Flowery Isles,” and was drawn to the idea of fabricating a whole collection around it. “The story itself is quite dark, but it ends in this soft, dream-like world with a floral abundance,” said Siriano, who saw this duality and tension as a challenge to touch upon the two sides of his personality—the sinister and the sweet.</p><p>The book deals with themes of vanity, vengeance, and rivalry (themes that frequently appear within the fashion world). Siriano approached this idea by giving his voluminous, dramatic designs a bit more edge this season. Things felt less precious. He opened the show with a series of black faux-leather pieces, all laser-cut, including trenches and gowns. They felt a bit macabre and heavy for a spring show, but Siriano can back this up by saying he was simply following the book.</p><p>This transitioned into a plentiful array of black tailoring looks, something Siriano has always done well for his diverse range of clients. (On the runway, he continues to be one of the few to commit to body diversity, too.) He showed cropped tuxedo suits for her and sexier laced-up trousers for him. “I used to do a lot of beautiful suits, and I wanted to show that again,” he said.</p><p>In the second half of the show, Siriano transitioned into more mythical and fantasyland territory, and a break from all the black. Tying back to the 18th century, he made use of florals and hand-embroidered tapestries that resembled something you’d find in a dusty historical chateau—only he exaggerated them even more, to Marie Antoinette levels with ruffles and three-dimensional bodices. “I’ve never used tapestries before, and that felt exciting to me,” he said. “I wanted to modernize it.” The problem was that some of the silhouettes were simply not modern. Does someone really want to wear a tulle-covered, corseted bodysuit with a sky-high neckline that blocks their vision? Fashion can and should be fun, but sometimes his ideas read as silly.</p><p>That’s not to suggest he shouldn’t embrace whimsy and maximalism. In fact, Siriano is at his best when he fully leans into extravagance and delivers a moment. It’s a fine line. His long, hot-pink maxi gown featured “upside down” tulip sleeves that would look fab on any red carpet. (All the phones in the crowd went up to snap that look—especially the celebs eyeing it for appearances.) Ditto the strapless, hand-painted sequin floral gowns he made or the black tulle explosion of a minidress that he gave structure with a corseted waist. These were extreme—but tastefully extreme. Taste is subjective, of course, and that’s another lesson you could probably learn in a bevy of fairytales.</p></div>",
